<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Ashraf Musa, Emir of Homs</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1263">1263</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Ashraf Musa was the last Ayyubid emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a>. He lost the city in 1248 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-yusuf">al-Nasir Yusuf</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, who gave him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turbessel">Tell Bashir</a> instead. Restored to Homs by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1260, he then joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> and helped defeat a Mongol army at the first Battle of Homs in December 1260. On his death Homs passed to the Mamluk sultanate.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Al-Ashraf Musa ibn al-Mansur Ibrahim ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirkuh">Shirkuh</a> was born in 1229. Unlike most Ayyubid princes, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a> descended from Asad al-Din Shirkuh, brother of Najm ad-Din Ayyub. Homs was one of the smaller principalities of the Ayyubid confederation and had gained weight under his father, al-Mansur Ibrahim, whose death in 1246 left it to al-Ashraf at the age of 17. In 1249 he married Amat al-Latif, daughter of a Damascus Hanbali scholar and spiritual adviser to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>'s sister, just released from prison; she died in 1253.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the spring of 1247 al-Ashraf sought the patronage of as-Salih Ayyub, sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, ceding him the stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamiyah">Salamiyah</a>. His fourth cousin an-Nasir Yusuf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> answered by moving to annex <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a>, and as-Salih's relief force was delayed by the sultan's illness. By mid-August 1248 al-Ashraf had surrendered the city, keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a> and al-Rahba on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and receiving the isolated outpost of Tall Bashir.</p><p>From Tall Bashir he made secret contact with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and encouraged their invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu Khan</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> in 1260, the Mongol general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitbuqa">Kitbuqa</a> made al-Ashraf viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and all Syria and restored him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a>. The historian Reuven Amitai judges the title nominal: he was told to rule from Homs, and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqta</a> of 100 horses matched a battalion commander's. On Mongol orders he led an expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a>, destroying its citadel wall and arsenal and selling its public library, though he left the city walls standing for fear of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> invasion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Al-Ashraf then reached a secret understanding with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutuz">Qutuz</a>, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ain-jalut">Battle of Ain Jalut</a> in September 1260 he withdrew his troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitbuqa">Kitbuqa</a>'s coalition, helping to swing the battle to the Mamluks. He lost the viceroyalty but kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a> as a Mamluk vassal. Baibars, sultan from October 1260, appointed Alam al-Din Sanjar al-Bashqirdi as deputy governor of Homs. Al-Ashraf died early in 1263 without heirs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Ashraf Musa, Emir of Homs” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Al-Ashraf Musa</b> lost Homs for a time to Aleppo.</blockquote><a href="/state/ayyubid-emirate-of-homs">Ayyubid Emirate of Homs</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.24&amp;year=1253" aria-label="Cairo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5361 1067 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Al-Ashraf Musa, Emir of Homs"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5811 1337h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5811 1337h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.24&amp;year=1253">What was here in 1253? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
